\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Flat planning versus boundary-guided segmentation. Top: one imagined rollout covers the full horizon and all actions are optimized jointly, so rollout error compounds without reset. Bottom: the detector fires at physical phase boundaries (Detection 1 and 2, dashed lines at unequal steps); each segment takes the boundary waypoint as its subgoal and is optimized on its own, and execution re-anchors to a fresh real observation at every boundary, clearing accumulated drift. Segment lengths follow detected events, not a fixed stride.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rollout}{{1}{2}{Flat planning versus boundary-guided segmentation. Top: one imagined rollout covers the full horizon and all actions are optimized jointly, so rollout error compounds without reset. Bottom: the detector fires at physical phase boundaries (Detection 1 and 2, dashed lines at unequal steps); each segment takes the boundary waypoint as its subgoal and is optimized on its own, and execution re-anchors to a fresh real observation at every boundary, clearing accumulated drift. Segment lengths follow detected events, not a fixed stride}{figure.1}{}}
